📥 Docker Image Commands
Pull an Image
docker pull nginx

Downloads the Nginx image from a configured registry such as Docker Hub.

List Images
docker images

or:

docker image ls
Remove an Image
docker rmi nginx

If the image is still referenced by existing containers, Docker may prevent removal until those references are handled.

Tag an Image
docker tag nginx mynginx:v1

Tags provide human-readable names and versions for images.
Inspect an Image
docker inspect nginx

Displays detailed image metadata.

View Image History
docker history nginx

Shows the image's layer history.
🚀 Running Containers
Run a Container
docker run nginx

This creates and starts a container from the Nginx image.

Run in Detached Mode
docker run -d nginx

The container runs in the background.
Assign a Name
docker run -d --name web nginx

Now the container can be referenced using:

web

instead of its generated container ID.

📋 List Containers
Running Containers
docker ps
All Containers
docker ps -a

This includes containers that have stopped.

🛑 Stop a Container
docker stop web
▶️ Start a Stopped Container
docker start web
🔄 Restart a Container
docker restart web
🗑️ Remove a Container
docker rm web

A running container normally needs to be stopped before removal.

Force removal:

docker rm -f web

Use force removal carefully.
📜 View Container Logs
docker logs web

Follow logs in real time:

docker logs -f web
🖥️ Execute Commands Inside a Container

Open an interactive shell:

docker exec -it web bash

If Bash is unavailable:

docker exec -it web sh

Run a single command:

docker exec web nginx -v
🔍 Inspect a Container
docker inspect web

This can provide information about:

Network configuration
Mounts
Environment
Container configuration
IP addresses
Runtime settings
⚙️ View Processes
docker top web

Displays processes running inside the container.

📊 View Resource Usage
docker stats

Displays live resource usage such as:

CPU
Memory
Network I/O
Block I/O
🌐 Port Mapping

Containers have their own network namespace.

To expose an Nginx container:

docker run -d -p 8080:80 --name web nginx

The mapping is:

Host Port 8080
      │
      ▼
Container Port 80
      │
      ▼
Nginx

Access:

http://localhost:8080

On a remote server:

http://<SERVER-IP>:8080

The server's firewall or cloud Security Group must also allow the required port.

